STEP 2 — TELL US WHAT'S HAPPENING

This is the most important interaction in the patient experience.

It should be designed extremely carefully.

Objective

Allow the patient to communicate their concern naturally, using either speech or text.

Voice should be prominent because it is a key part of MedFlow.

Screen composition

Heading:

"What brings you in today?"

Supporting text:

"Tell us in your own words. Take your time."

Keep the screen visually clean.

Do not show long technical descriptions, multiple example cards, database terminology or AI explanations.

Input mode selection

Give the patient two simple ways to continue:

- Speak
- Type

Use a polished segmented selector or another appropriate existing design-system component.

Both modes must be easy to discover.

Voice should receive stronger visual emphasis without hiding the typing option.

VOICE EXPERIENCE

When Speak is selected, make the recording interaction the centrepiece of the screen.

Create a large, beautiful circular recording control.

It should be the main visual element.

The circle should be centred and generously sized, with an appropriate icon and visual feedback.

Do not use an ordinary rectangular record button.

Idle state

Display:

A large circular microphone button.

Below it:

"Tap to start speaking"

Small supporting text:

"Speak naturally. You can review your words before submitting."

The circle must clearly communicate that it is interactive.

Recording state

When the patient starts recording:

- The circle changes into a clear recording state.
- Show a recording indicator.
- Display elapsed recording time.
- Provide an obvious Stop Recording action.
- Use subtle animated rings, waveform feedback or another design-system-compatible indicator.
- Do not use distracting animation.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.

The patient must always understand whether recording is active.

Stopping state

After the patient stops recording:

- Display a short transcribing state.
- Make it clear that MedFlow is processing the audio.
- Prevent accidental repeated submissions.
- Provide a sensible timeout and error state.

Transcript review state

After successful transcription, transition the screen into a clean transcript editor.

Heading:

"Here's what we heard."

Supporting text:

"Review your words and make any corrections before continuing."

Display the transcript in a beautiful, editable text area.

The transcript must be editable.

Provide two clear actions:

- Record Again
- Continue

If the patient chooses Record Again, start a fresh recording without losing other intake details.

Do not expose the internal "transcript_raw", model name, model ID, audio MIME details or backend processing information.

Preserve all required raw and confirmed transcript data internally.

The patient should see only what they need.

Microphone unavailable or denied

If recording is not possible:

Display a friendly, non-technical message.

Suggested copy:

"Microphone access isn't available right now. You can type your concern instead."

Provide an obvious route to the Type experience.

Never trap the patient in voice mode.

TYPED EXPERIENCE

When Type is selected, display a clean writing interface.

Heading:

"Write what brings you in."

Supporting text:

"Tell us what you've been experiencing in your own words."

Use a generous, readable text area with a clear placeholder:

"Start typing here..."

A subtle writing hint may say:

"You can include when it started, how it feels and anything else you want the care team to know."

Do not make the field look like a dense medical questionnaire.

Do not split the patient's story into separate symptom questions.

Keep the patient's ability to describe their concern freely.

Provide:

- Continue
- Switch to Speak

The patient must be able to change modes without losing text already entered.

IMPORTANT: REMOVE THESE FROM THE NORMAL PATIENT INTERFACE

Remove the visible synthetic patient example selector from the normal patient workflow.

Remove:

- Example 1, Example 2, Example 3.
- Clear fields control.
- Original raw voice transcript display.
- Model identification.
- Technical transcription explanations.
- Repeated instructions.
- Excessive helper text.
- Long descriptions of the backend process.

These details either belong in development/testing utilities or internal documentation.

Do not remove the underlying test fixtures or synthetic scenarios from the repository.

Input validation

- Do not allow an empty concern to proceed.
- Preserve typed content when switching modes.
- Preserve transcript edits.
- Preserve the raw transcription separately in the existing data model.
- Do not automatically alter a patient's confirmed wording.
- Continue to enforce existing character limits and server-side validation.

